          ],
          "content": "Ganita Manjari | Grade 9 | Part I"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.108,
            0.886,
            0.168
          ],
          "content": "For equilateral triangles, the ratio of perimeter to the side is 3:1. As the side of the equilateral triangle gets larger (or smaller), the ratio of perimeter to side stays fixed at 3:1."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.123,
            0.177,
            0.513,
            0.3
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.286,
                0.307,
                0.354,
                0.325
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 6.5"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_2_image_3.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.523,
            0.182,
            0.877,
            0.26
          ],
          "content": "What about a circle? What is its perimeter (usually called the **circumference**) in terms of its diameter?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.523,
            0.259,
            0.877,
            0.339
          ],
          "content": "Is the ratio of circumference (C) to diameter (D) the same for circles of all sizes (Fig. 6.5)? What do you think?"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.362,
            0.713,
            0.386
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "6.2 Perimeter of a Circle —The C/D Ratio"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.396,
            0.886,
            0.455
          ],
          "content": "In ancient days people realised that the ratio of the circumference to the diameter of the circle does not change if we change the size of the circle."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.457,
            0.644,
            0.479
          ],
          "content": "Let’s call this ratio the ‘C/D ratio’ of the circle."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.481,
            0.528,
            0.502
          ],
          "content": "What is the value of the C/D ratio?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.504,
            0.541,
            0.527
          ],
          "content": "How would you estimate this ratio?"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.325,
            0.549,
            0.667,
            0.574
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "HOME MEASUREMENT"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.129,
            0.586,
            0.861,
            0.735
          ],
          "content": "You can do a simple measurement at home to estimate the C/D ratio. Take a cotton reel with thin thread around it. Measure the diameter D of the reel as accurately as possible. Unwrap and then tightly wrap the thread around the reel 20 times. Unwrap it again; measure its length L, and calculate $\\frac { L } { 2 0 . D }$ This is the ratio we want. For accuracy, the thread should be very thin. Please do the experiment! Do you get a ratio between 3 and 4? Between 3.1 and 3.2?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.13,
            0.738,
            0.86,
            0.777
          ],
          "content": "It is also possible to estimate the C/D ratio using pure geometry, i.e., without any measurements at all! Can you imagine how?"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.806,
            0.863,
            0.852
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "C/D’s Adventurous Journey: From Ancient Approximations to the Exact Formula of Mādhava"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.858,
            0.886,
            0.9
          ],
          "content": "Mathematicians have been fascinated by circles, and the C/D ratio, since ancient times and across geographical regions. This constant,"
        }
